#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133400 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40486856-137f-55c4-8464-f99e7e1c659f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "1b09be02-1515-5277-bac6-9289b9dbe371" :question/math-academy-id "e-11506" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}} 2y\\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right] \\mathrm{d}A$,\ngiven that $D$ is the region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z=0$, $x= 1$ and the line $z =x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}2ydy]dA &= ∬_(D)[y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = \\sqrt{x}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)xdA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate this double integral, we plot $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2142/Source/Images/0049c315f956b65694eb3d93cfd89aeb.png)\n\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)xdA &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}xdzdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x[z]_{z = 0}^{z = x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf29db1b-f47b-53de-b31d-e4da1d0a9e5f" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "216b16aa-d279-519e-a749-b9fd5517a6db" :question/math-academy-id "e-11507" :question/problem "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x$\nwhere $R$ is the type III region defined as\n$R=\\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x \\leq z \\leq 2x+2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq z+xz \\right\\}$." :question/worked-solution "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: u_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq u_2(x,z) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2142/Source/Images/6b616094dd63c84d3421f6a60ebfd54a.png)\n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\int_{u_1(x,z)}^{u_2(x,z)} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d y \\right] \\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu_1(x,z) = 0, \\qquad u_2(x,z) = z+xz\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{S} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{z+xz} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD =\\left\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x \\leq z \\leq 2x+2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{z + xz}f(x, y, z)dy]dA &= ∫_{-3}^{3}∫_{x}^{2x + 2}[∫_{0}^{z + xz}f(x, y, z)dy]dzdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-3}^{3}∫_{x}^{2x + 2}∫_{0}^{z + xz}f(x, y, z)dydzdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4693bb81-f299-50fa-9cbd-3a5bb1c90da9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type III Region"}] :question/id #uuid "72b1ac41-be39-5a51-bc0c-738d36cdb81b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12378" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 8xy \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt x \\right\\}$,\nand $D$ is the finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the line $x=1$, and the line $z=x$ in the first quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 8xy \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}} 8xy\\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right ] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}8xydy]dA &= ∬_(D)4x[y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = \\sqrt{x}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4x(x - 0)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4x^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2142/Source/Images/aeb7a9800589495e7e5dac34dce55823.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)4x^{2}dA &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}4x^{2}dzdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4x^{2}[z]_{z = 0}^{z = x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4x^{2}(x - 0)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= [x^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= 1^{4} - 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1889f67-327c-5e6e-b646-5bd58a244778" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Evaluating Triple Integrals Over Type III Regions From Diagrams"}] :question/id #uuid "21fdb746-8831-5b97-acf0-5a14a2e34980" :question/math-academy-id "e-12400" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 36z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2142/Source/Images/52cbbd44402aa96efd677c6849e17960.png)\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2142/Source/Images/75dd44f587bfca01c1e43dd033fc216d.png)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 5-x-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2142/Source/Images/8c33c91fefc6d1ab75b1cfc77aa64fa2.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 5-x-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 36z^2 \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{5-x-z} 36z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{5 - x - z}36z^{2}dydzdx &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{5 - x - z}36z^{2}dy]dzdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}36z^{2}[y]_{y = 0}^{y = 5 - x - z}dzdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}36z^{2}(5 - x - z)dzdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(180z^{2} - 36xz^{2} - 36z^{3})dzdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(180z^{2} - 36xz^{2} - 36z^{3})dzdx &= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{1}(180z^{2} - 36xz^{2} - 36z^{3})dz]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[60z^{3} - 12xz^{3} - 9z^{4}]_{z = 0}^{z = 1}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(51 - 12x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}(51 - 12x)dx &= [51x - 6x^{2}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= (51(1) - 6(1)^{2}) - 0 \\\\\n&= 45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1889f67-327c-5e6e-b646-5bd58a244778" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Evaluating Triple Integrals Over Type III Regions From Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a33d0c50-02e9-5042-b0a2-84a1640d59e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130858} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b99ae5-c0b2-5a55-9940-c43d2b6c906a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130859} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cec2da-b390-5844-9f8a-814ab08f1913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130860} {:answer/id #uuid "3d52e0c0-7dc5-5680-894f-1c23e4df29d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130861} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebcbdd1-f859-5817-a4bd-adae53ee860f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ebcbdd1-f859-5817-a4bd-adae53ee860f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130862} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1a300fd-ce2c-5bf6-9b45-436b1c9be020" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130857}] :question/id #uuid "dac9f414-d986-53df-9bd2-235db5304432" :question/math-academy-id "q-161480" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2142/Source/Images/q-161480.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)12xzdV$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40486856-137f-55c4-8464-f99e7e1c659f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93d895fa-8fb9-56f1-83ce-38b1d5116a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131715} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6b29f0-8b3e-58e6-b6b5-9ba07c4baf4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131716} {:answer/id #uuid "b63a2262-6a17-5749-a985-2555f2e25135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131717} {:answer/id #uuid "3536dd71-abe2-5793-a75e-3d8ca1be6a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131718} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8f45fc-cdb7-53c0-a5d5-b940ab4c1b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b63a2262-6a17-5749-a985-2555f2e25135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131717} :answer-field/id #uuid "867f8dc6-d50f-555c-99ae-7e2162f287a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131714}] :question/id #uuid "e6d26c08-ceec-5b48-a4a3-f3df87eec565" :question/math-academy-id "q-40403" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{x}2xyzdy]dA$\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n$D = {(x, z): 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{4 - 2z}, 0 \\le z \\le 2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40486856-137f-55c4-8464-f99e7e1c659f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f66adb06-44ad-563b-ac24-9ab6372d2035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131727} {:answer/id #uuid "08cdccd8-37ae-5add-81cd-fccd7a6665b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131728} {:answer/id #uuid "1993ae02-9b3f-5000-958c-8aa3b5491aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #